 languages.", "type": "text"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Take Away", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}], "type": "learning_skills"}], "id": 12, "type": "DWS"}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}], "type": "lab_options"}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "audio", "title": "", "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n4le01ob02re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "100", "text": "<strong>Mr. Peterson</strong>: Good morning. Since we didn't get to finish last class, today we're going to continue with the topic: History of programming languages. Does anyone know something about it?</br>\n<strong>Sophie</strong>: In the 20th century, the first computers used to have poor limited speed and memory capacity. But then, it was discovered that logic could be represented with numbers and not only with words, as it used to be.", "image": "enpe7n4le01ob02re2img01.jpg", "id": 0}, {"time": "25300", "text": "<strong>Mr. Peterson</strong>: You're right, Sophie. That's not the first fact we know about programming languages, though. Its history didn't start in the 20th century; everything started in 1890 when Herman Hollerith encoded census data on punch cards. He managed to apply this when he saw how train conductors used to encode the arrival of people; back then, they marked the position by punching holes on the tickets. Now, Sophie, do you think our forebears could forecast how programming would be in the future?</br>\n<strong>Sophie</strong>: Well, I guess they didn't think it could ever be so important for us nowadays. Then, what were the first programming languages? Was it Basic Language?", "image": "enpe7n4le01ob02re2img02.jpg", "id": 1}, {"time": "71000", "text": "<strong>Mr. Peterson</strong>: No. One of the oldest programming languages was FORTRAN, which stands for formula translation. It was used since 1957 to perform scientific, mathematical, and statistical computations. The key features of FORTRAN are currently used in aerospace, automotive industries, government, and research institutions. In addition, COBOL, which stands for Common Business Oriented Language, developed in 1959 just after FORTRAN. COBOL was designed to be user-friendly and it used to be applied by the United States Postal Service. </br>\n<strong>Sophie</strong>: Then, Basic Language didn't initiate until 1964.  Created as a simplified language, it was designed for those who didn't have a technical background to avoid making intellectual effort.</br>\n<strong>Mr. Peterson</strong>: Correct.", "image": "enpe7n4le01ob02re2img03.jpg", "id": 2}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n4le01ob02re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}]}